#basePath P2_Lampricide
#include "Header.leaf"

:: Start
@trigger    OnLocationChanged
// "NONE" ensures no other threads are in-progress
// global:currentPhase == 2 ensures player is in phase 2
@when       global:currentThread == "NONE", global:currentPhase == 2, location == "FieldStationLampricide"
@cutscene
// Lock all location on map
$call SetInThreadLocks()
$call DisableMapButton()

// Mark start of thread
$set global:currentThread = "P2_Lampricide"

{@}   
       Paul had mentioned something called lampricide, and a quick call to Renée confirmed that the Field Station used to apply lampricide. 
       So here you are, on the shore of one of the lake's many tributaries, being attacked by a horde of mosquitoes. 

{@Claire}
       Isn't this WONDERFUL?!

{@}
       At your side, the Field Station's engineer, Claire, looks out at the water like a mother would her child. 

{@You}
       I'm not sure if wonderful is the word I'd use...

{@Claire}
       Ah, but the mosquitoes are part of the experience! Here, put on more bug repellant! 
       We must be valiant towards diseases spread by insects like mosquitoes and ticks!

{@}
       She covers you in a thick layer of repellant and ensures your legs are properly covered by your pants and socks. 

{@Claire}
       Excellent! We are PREPARED! Now, what has brought you to my domain, young scientist?!

{@You}
       Um, I'm here to ask you about something called lampricide?

{@Claire}
       Yes! LAMPRICIDE! We used to apply it to the tributaries around the lake!
       
{@You}
       So, like, what </i>is</i> lampricide?

{@Claire}
       Lampricide is a pesticide that kills sea lamprey larvae but doesn't affect other fish! Hmm. Firstly, you must understand the life cycle of sea lamprey! 
       Sea lamprey eggs are laid in tributaries, which are small rivers that feed into the lake!
       Once the eggs hatch, sea lamprey spend the first four to five years as larvae buried in the riverbed, eating organic material that floats past them!
       After maturing, the sea lamprey then swim towards the lake and spend 12-16 months there before returning to the tributary to spawn and die!

{@You}
       So this lampricide is only effective when the sea lamprey are in this larvae stage?

{@Claire}
       PRECISELY, young scientist! And it is very effective at lowering the population of sea lamprey! //evidence chip

{@You}
       You do know that you can't be more than, like, five years older than me?

{@Claire}
       We are ALL young at heart! I, too, am a young scientist!

{@You}
       Hmm. Well, if lampricide is so effective, why hasn't it been used recently?

{@}
       Claire's demeanor becomes more serious. 

{@Claire}
       After the pandemic, our lampricide program ended, and no new funding for lampricide has been given. 
       It's important to remember that not everyone wants to add chemicals to the water, and people have their worries about lampricide. 
       Though lampricide is safe, it's also important to respect the wishes of people like indigenous populations who would rather use other methods. 
       We are all working together to create a balanced ecosystem, so we have to decide on the next steps together and consult with the community. //evidence chip

{@You}
       Hm. I can go to the town hall later today and ask whether the town will give money for lampricide. 
       That way everyone will have a say in the next steps.  

{@}
       Claire smiles. 

{@Claire}
       Yes! That is an excellent idea, young scientist!

{@You}
       Thanks, young scientist. 

// TODO: Rest of Field Station section here

// TODO: Give evidence chip
// $call GiveEvidenceChip([Evidence])

$call UnlockLocation(TownHall)
$call EnableMapButton()


:: AtTownHall
@trigger    OnLocationChanged
// global:currentThread ensures the correct thread
// global:currentPhase == 2 ensures player is in phase 2
@when       global:currentThread == "P2_Lampricide", global:currentPhase == 2, location == "TownHall"
// Lock all location on map
$call SetInThreadLocks()
$call DisableMapButton()

{@}   
       The town hall meeting has just started when you slip inside the main chamber. 
       At the front, a young woman you recognize as Mayor Avery Byrde sits alongside Paul, the DNR representative, and your boss Saoirse. 

{@Mayor}
       Hello, everyone, and thank you for coming today.
       I'll open up the floor to any community members who'd like to speak. 

{@}
       Hands immediately go up, questions about funding for the middle school and zoning updates near the city center. 
       After a few minutes, it's your turn. 
       
{@You}
       Um, hi. I'm Ana Almeidas. I grew up here, and I currently work for Sea Grant. 
       We're currently looking into different methods of controlling sea lamprey in the Great Lakes.
       An option that's been used in the past is lampricide, which is a chemical that's put in the water.

{@}
       There's a murmur through the crowd at the word "chemical".

{@You}
       Lampricide is safe and very effective, but we would need funding for the program. 
       We'd like to ask for that funding and permission from the community to use it.

{@Mayor}
       I can see there are some questions in the crowd, so we'll go through those first. 

{@Fisher}
       So my entire business relies on there being fish in the lakes. 
       How do you know this chemical isn't gonna kill everything and not just the lamprey?

{@You}
       There have been plenty of studies done on how lampricide works. 
       It only affects sea lamprey and won't hurt any of the other fish. 
       And by controlling the sea lamprey population, the other fish you need will be protected. 

{@Grandparent}
       But what about people? I don't want my grandkids swimming in chemical water. 

{@You}
       We also know that water that's been treated with lampricide is safe for people. 
       Lampricide breaks down naturally in the water, so unlike something like lead, it doesn't accumulate. 
       //i'd like to add something about indigenous worries over lampricide, but i think i need more information to do it respectfully

{@Citizen}//idk what to call this person
       You mentioned funding. How much would that be?

{@Ana}
       Well, I can't really give you a particular number right now. 
       I know the city has a lot of services, and it's important to find a balance with everything.
       But sea lamprey can pose an economic risk when their population isn't regulated.
       By not doing anytthing now, we risk having to spend more money in the future to fix the lamprey population. 

{@Mayor}
       Thank you, everyone, for your thoughtful questions. 
       Ana, the city council will think about it after this meeting, and we'll let you know soon. 

{@}
       You nod and go back to watching the rest of the townhall. 
       After the closed hearing for the city council, the Mayor approaches you.

{@Mayor}
       Ana, right? Thank you for coming tonight. 

{@You}
       Thanks for letting me talk. Any word from the city council?

{@Mayor}
       They've approved preliminary funding for lampricide use in municipal tributaries. 
       It's only for a year right now, but if you show success, that can be reevaluated. 

{@You}
       Oh, that's a relief. I don't know what I would've told my boss otherwise. 

{@Mayor}
       Saoirse? She definitely helped during the city council meeting. 
       She spoke highly of you, too. 

{@You}
       Really? This is literally my first day. 

{@Mayor}
       Call it a good vibe. I see it. 
       We've got high hopes for you and this program. 
       Not to say the city's hopes are riding on your shoulders, but...

{@You}
       Yeah, I know. 

{@}
       She smiles and steps away. 
       You're left watching the rest of the town walk out of the townhall. 
       You think about their private dreams and hopes, all rushing down the town's streets to the lake. 
       The photo of the sea lamprey mocks you from your pocket. It nips at your ears. 

{@Lamprey}
       There's no time like the present for me. 
       When I'm done, where will the past you remember be?

{@}
       You rip the photo in half. 
       ...
       You regret it.

